::: {.doc-subject}

Calculate k-core decomposition (coreness for each node).
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L65369-L65496){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.coreness(
    attr_name=None,
    return_dict=False,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Coreness](../assets/graphs/coreness.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="The eight-node graph with each node colored by its core number: the dense cluster A, B, C, D has coreness 3 (accent), the ring E, F, G coreness 2 (teal), and the pendant H coreness 1 (violet). The table lists the nodes at each coreness value." width="100%"}

The k-core is the maximal subgraph where every node has degree >= k. A node's coreness is
the highest k-value for which it belongs to the k-core. Uses iterative pruning: repeatedly
removes nodes with degree < k until all remaining nodes have degree >= k.

Higher coreness indicates nodes in denser, more cohesive parts of the graph. The k-core
decomposition is useful for finding the 'nucleus' of a network.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[attr_name]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Name of the node attribute to store coreness values. If `None`, uses `coreness` as the
    attribute name.

<code>[return_dict]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True`, returns a dictionary mapping node IDs to coreness values. If `False`, stores
    values as node attributes and returns the Graph for chaining.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [dict[int, int] or Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `return_dict=` is `True`: Dictionary mapping node IDs to coreness values
    (non-negative integers). If `return_dict=` is `False`: Returns self for method chaining.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~k_core`) 

:   The subgraph of the nodes with coreness at least `k`.

[](`~k_shell`) 

:   The subgraph of the nodes with coreness exactly `k`.

[](`~k_truss`) 

:   A stricter core, where each edge must lie in triangles.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

In a complete graph on four nodes, every node keeps three neighbors however the graph is
peeled, so all four have coreness 3. A pendant node hanging off the cluster has only one
neighbor, so its coreness is 1:

::: {#5a0b5864 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("A", "C"), ("A", "D"), ("B", "C"), ("B", "D"),
     ("C", "D"), ("D", "E")]
)

label = graph.get_label_from_id
{label(n): k for n, k in graph.coreness(return_dict=True).items()}
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
{'E': 1, 'C': 3, 'A': 3, 'D': 3, 'B': 3}
```
:::
:::


Without `return_dict=True`, the values go in the `coreness` node attribute:

::: {#307f947f .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
spots = {
    "A": (1, 1), "B": (3, 1), "C": (0, 2.6), "D": (4, 2.6), "E": (5, 1),
}
graph.set_node_attrs(
    "pos", {graph.get_id_from_label(v): p for v, p in spots.items()}
)

graph.coreness().plot(
    layout="pos", color_by="coreness", label_by="label", height=280
)
```

